a("Where is Grand Forks and what is the city known for?",
"<p>Grand Forks is the seat of Grand Forks County, on the western bank of the Red River of the North, where the Red Lake River joins it. The name refers to that fork. The city covers about 29.3 square miles at an elevation of roughly 837 feet, 74 miles north of Fargo-Moorhead and 145 miles south of Winnipeg.</p>"
"<p>East Grand Forks, Minnesota, sits across the river, and the two cities form a metro area of 104,184 people in the 2020 census. A seller in Grand Forks deals with North Dakota rules and the Grand Forks County offices, while a seller across the river deals with Minnesota.</p>"
"<p>The city has no lakes within its limits. The Red River and English Coulee cross it, and the old floodplain is now the Greater Grand Forks Greenway.</p>")
a("How did Grand Forks get started?",
"<p>Grand Forks began as a fur-trading site that French traders called Les Grandes Fourches, and a U.S. post office opened there on June 15, 1870. Steamboat captain Alexander Griggs platted the town in 1875, and it was incorporated on February 22, 1881.</p>"
"<p>The University of North Dakota was established in 1883, six years before statehood. The Great Northern Railway arrived in 1880 and the Northern Pacific in 1887. In 1954 the city was chosen as the site of an Air Force base.</p>"
"<p>Population reached 59,166 in the 2020 census, up 12.0 percent from 52,838 in 2010. The median age is 30.2, which fits a university and military town.</p>")
a("Who employs people in Grand Forks?",
"<p>The University of North Dakota is the largest employer in the metro area, and Altru Health System is the largest private employer. The city's 2024 annual financial report, as summarized on Wikipedia, gives these counts.</p>"
"<table><thead><tr><th>Employer</th><th>Employees</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>University of North Dakota</td><td>5,562</td></tr><tr><td>Altru Health System</td><td>3,400</td></tr><tr><td>Grand Forks Air Force Base</td><td>2,897</td></tr><tr><td>Grand Forks Public Schools</td><td>1,700</td></tr></tbody></table>"
"<p>The economy also includes manufacturing and food processing, and the state-owned North Dakota Mill and Elevator is described as the nation's largest flour mill.</p>")
a("Which schools and roads serve Grand Forks?",
"<p>Grand Forks Public Schools enrolls about 7,400 students and runs ten elementary schools, the Twining combined school, three middle schools (Schroeder, South and Valley), and two high schools, Central and Red River. A nine-member elected board governs the district.</p>"
"<p>US 2, Interstate 29 and US 81 serve the city. Grand Forks International Airport has Delta service to Minneapolis-St. Paul and Allegiant flights to Mesa, Sanford and Las Vegas. Amtrak's Empire Builder stops daily, and Cities Area Transit, running since 1926, operates 12 bus routes.</p>"
"<ul><li>I-29 and US 81 serve the city, and US 2 crosses it</li><li>Delta runs daily round trips to Minneapolis-St. Paul</li><li>Amtrak's Empire Builder stops daily</li></ul>")
a("What does a Grand Forks winter and spring mean for a house?",
"<p>Grand Forks has a warm-summer humid continental climate, with January daily highs averaging 15.8 degrees Fahrenheit and July highs averaging 80.7 degrees. The record low is minus 43 degrees from January 1912.</p>"
"<p>Average precipitation is 21.74 inches a year and average snowfall is 48.4 inches. A tight envelope, a sound roof and a working furnace carry more weight in a Grand Forks inspection than they would in a milder place.</p>"
"<p>Spring is the other season to plan around. Melting snow feeds the Red River, which leads to the story every Grand Forks homeowner knows.</p>")
a("How did the 1997 flood change Grand Forks housing?",
"<p>The 1997 Red River flood did more damage in Grand Forks than in any other major valley city that year, and a downtown fire destroyed 11 buildings at the flood's peak. The peak flow was about 135,000 cubic feet per second, according to the city.</p>"
"<table><thead><tr><th>Flood protection fact</th><th>City figure</th></tr></thead><tbody>"
"<tr><td>Voluntary buyouts (1997-1998)</td><td>850 properties</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Dwellings demolished</td><td>694</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Houses moved</td><td>161</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>English Coulee diversion channel</td><td>9.5 miles</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>New pump stations</td><td>12</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Project cost, Grand Forks and East Grand Forks</td><td>$409 million</td></tr></tbody></table>"
"<p>Levee tops are about 10 feet wide, and floodwalls add 3 feet. The city says the system could handle a 500-year flood by adding clay to the levees. About 20 miles of greenway trails and a golf course now occupy the former floodplain.</p>"
"<p>For sellers, flood history is part of the conversation with any buyer, so know where your lot sits relative to the levee system.</p>")
a("Who values and who bills property tax in Grand Forks?",
"<p>The City of Grand Forks Assessing Department values property inside the city, and the Grand Forks County Treasurer bills and collects the tax. City Assessor Tami Lazur leads the department, which also appraises new construction each year and checks exemption and credit claims.</p>"
"<p>The city inspects on a six-year cycle, reaching about one-sixth of properties each year. In an August 2025 Grand Forks Herald report, the assessor said owners may decline interior inspections, in which case the office judges from the outside. She summed that up as judging the whole book by its cover.</p>"
"<p>The assessing office is at City Hall, 255 N 4th Street. Email is GFAssessing@grandforksgov.com.</p>")
a("What are the Grand Forks property tax dates and penalties?",
"<p>Statements are mailed by December 26. Taxes are due January 1 and can be paid without penalty until March 1, and a 5 percent discount applies to full payment by February 15.</p>"
"<table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Rule under N.D.C.C. 57-20-01</th></tr></thead><tbody>"
"<tr><td>February 15</td><td>5% discount if the full year is paid</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>March 1</td><td>First installment due; 3% penalty after</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>May 1</td><td>Additional 3%</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>July 1</td><td>Additional 3%</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>October 15</td><td>Second installment due; first-installment penalty grows by 3%; second installment carries 6% after</td></tr></tbody></table>"
"<p>The county treasurer's mailing address is PO Box 5638, Grand Forks ND 58206-5638, and the phone is 701-780-8292. Online lookups run through the county's Tyler iTax and Vanguard property search pages.</p>")
a("What are special assessments in Grand Forks?",
"<p>Special assessments are charges that split the cost of an infrastructure project among the properties that benefit, and the city says they are not property taxes. They can fund sidewalks, street paving and lighting, water main, storm sewer, sanitary sewer and flood protection.</p>"
"<table><thead><tr><th>Project type</th><th>Repayment term</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Water mains, sanitary sewer, storm sewer</td><td>25 years</td></tr><tr><td>Paving (asphalt or concrete)</td><td>25 years</td></tr><tr><td>Street lighting, sidewalks, flood control</td><td>20 years</td></tr><tr><td>Gravel or maintenance streets</td><td>5 to 10 years</td></tr></tbody></table>"
"<p>Questions go to the city Finance and Administrative Services Department, 255 N 4th Street, at 701-746-2671. Ask for the remaining balance before listing, since buyers and lenders will want the payoff figure. Flood control is one of the project types the city lists.</p>")
a("How do I appeal a Grand Forks assessment?",
"<p>Appeal first to the city, which must hold a Board of Equalization meeting under state law, and written, phone or electronic appeals count as appearing in person. Written appeals go to tlazur@grandforksgov.com or PO Box 5200, Grand Forks ND 58206.</p>"
"<ul><li>City board: first 15 days of April</li><li>County board: first 10 days of June</li><li>State board: second Tuesday in August, only if you appeared at the city and county boards</li><li>Abatement: by November 1 of the year after the tax becomes payable</li></ul>"
"<p>Gather comparable sales, an appraisal or repair bids before the April meeting.</p>")
a("How does selling a Grand Forks house with tax owing work?",
"<p>Unpaid property tax does not block a sale, because the title company pays the county from your proceeds at closing. Ask for a payoff figure that includes any special assessments and penalties.</p>"
"<p>Deeds are recorded at the Grand Forks County Recorder's Office, 151 South 4th Street, phone 701-780-8262. The Recorder's page states that North Dakota has no deed or mortgage tax, and it accepts electronic recording through Simplifile, CSC Recording Solutions, Indecomm and ePN.</p>"
"<p>State law lets a county foreclose a tax lien after taxes go unpaid into the third year, and an owner can stop it by paying everything owed by October 1. The county website posts a Notice of Foreclosure of Tax Lien each year. If you are behind, selling to a cash buyer before the notice arrives keeps options open. We buy Grand Forks houses as they are, and the title company handles the county payoff.</p>")
